    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Serbian Empire emerged from the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>, son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-decanski">Stefan Dečanski</a> (r. 1322–1331), held the title of young king and ruled the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>, as Serbian heirs traditionally did. With the support of most of the nobility, Archbishop Danilo and generals such as Jovan Oliver Grčinić, he besieged his father at Nerodimlje and forced him to give up the throne; the old king died imprisoned in the fortress of Zvečan.</p><p>In 1333 Dušan attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-iii-palaiologos">Andronikos III Palaiologos</a> with the help of the Byzantine general Syrgian, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a> and Kastoria. By 1345 his state covered half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, more than either the Byzantine Empire or the Second Bulgarian Empire. That year at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a> he proclaimed himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a>, and on 16 April 1346 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> he was crowned Emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. The ceremony was performed by the newly elevated Serbian Patriarch Joanikije II, the Bulgarian Patriarch Simeon and Nicholas, Archbishop of Ohrid. His son Uroš was crowned King of Serbs and Greeks with nominal rule over the Serbian lands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dušan gave strict orders against piracy and for the safety of travellers and foreign merchants. Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> were resumed, and the port of Ragusa (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>) became an important point of exchange; Ragusan merchants held trading privileges throughout the realm. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-roads">Roman roads</a> such as the Via Militaris, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a>, the Via de Zenta and the Kopaonik road carried wine, manufactures and luxury goods from the coast and metals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, wool, skins and leather from the interior.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srebrenica">Srebrenica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudnik">Rudnik</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trepca">Trepča</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novo-brdo">Novo Brdo</a>, Kopaonik, Majdanpek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brskovo">Brskovo</a> and Samokov were centres for iron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, lead, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold. The silver mines provided much of the royal income; they were worked by slave labour and managed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, a colony of whom worked Novo Brdo. The currency was the dinar, also called perper after the Byzantine hyperpyron, and the imperial tax was one dinar per house a year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> was the most prosperous and densely populated region. Society had an aristocratic basis: nobles held hereditary estates worked by dependent sebri, peasants owing labour services.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Serbian tactics relied on wedge-shaped attacks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a> on the flanks. The main strength of the army was its heavily armoured knights, alongside hussars, light cavalry armed mainly with spears and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbows</a> for scouting, raiding and skirmishing. Many foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> served, mostly Germans as cavalry and Spaniards as infantry, and the emperor had a personal guard of German knights. The German nobleman Palman commanded this Alemannic Guard from 1331 and led all the mercenaries.</p><p>Dušan seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, Albania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and most of Macedonia from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, winning by sieges rather than field battles. In October 1350 he invaded Hum with an army said to number 80,000, against the Bosnian Ban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-ii-kotromanic">Stephen II Kotromanić</a>, who avoided battle and retreated to the mountains. Serbian armies reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomislavgrad">Duvno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetina">Cetina</a>, the Krka and Imotski, and some Bosnian nobles submitted, but most Bosnian fortresses held out. In 1355 Dušan was preparing new campaigns in the south and east when he died. Under his son the empire could not repel the Ottoman Turks, who defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> under Vukašin at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a> in 1371.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The emperors were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> (1346–1355) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a> (1355–1371); Vukašin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> was co-ruler with the title of king from 1365, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-marko">Prince Marko</a> was designated heir as young king from 1369. The monarch had broad powers but was advised by a permanent council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> and prelates, and the court, chancellery and administration were rough copies of those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusans-code">Dušan's Code</a> was enacted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> on 21 May 1349 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a> in 1354 by assemblies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, nobles and provincial governors. Its 201 articles rested on Roman-Byzantine law and on St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>'s Nomocanon of 1219; articles 172 and 174, on judicial independence, were taken from the Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilika">Basilika</a>. The Code listed the hierarchy as lands, cities, župas and krajištes, the krajištes being frontier župas, and it replaced the title of župan with the Greek-derived kefalija. Dušan raised the Serbian Archbishopric to the Serbian Patriarchate. Influenced by the clergy, he treated Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> with severity: converts to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> were condemned to the mines and its propagators threatened with death.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under Dušan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> was one of the most powerful states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the most powerful in Southeast Europe, a multi-ethnic and multi-lingual empire from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>. Its imperial title claimed the Byzantine succession, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> received it with indignation. The Bulgarian Empire under Ivan Alexander, whose sister Helena married Dušan, appears to have supported the coronation and the creation of the Patriarchate of Peć. Dušan also gave sanctuary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-kantakouzenos">John VI Kantakouzenos</a>, the former Byzantine regent then in revolt, and allied with him.</p><p>His severity towards Catholics worried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a>, which roused the rivalry of the Catholic Hungarians. Schools and monasteries enjoyed royal favour and became institutions that carried on Serbian traditions, and architecture, frescoes and mosaics of the period, influenced by Italian art, survive as its record. The 1339 map of Angelino Dulcert shows a flag with a red double-headed eagle above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> for Dušan's realm. During the centuries of Ottoman rule that followed, the memory of the Serbian Empire became an integral part of Serbian national identity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dušan died suddenly in December 1355 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a>, called the Weak, an epithet that also described the empire as it slid into feudal anarchy. The holdings of a rapid conquest had not been consolidated, and Uroš could neither repel foreign enemies nor check the independence of his nobles. The empire broke into a conglomeration of principalities, some of which did not even nominally acknowledge him. Meanwhile the Ottoman Turks spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, taking Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and then the other Balkan states.</p><p>In 1371 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> under Vukašin at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a>, where much of the nobility was killed, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the southern governors; Uroš died childless on 4 December 1371. With no agreed heir, semi-independent provincial lords ruled. Some successors claimed the imperial title in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> until 1402, but the lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> were never recovered. Moravian Serbia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar Hrebeljanović</a> became the strongest successor and fought the Ottomans at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1389. Serbia was annexed by the Ottomans in 1459.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Serbian Empire was the realm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> built by sieges and crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> in 1346, an Orthodox empire from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a> governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusans-code">Dušan's Code</a>. After his death in 1355 it fragmented under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a> and ended in 1371, the year of the Ottoman victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a>.</em></p>

